// detached-work.proto — Detached work as a MESSAGE PAYLOAD: its typed per-kind
// updates, and the update envelope that addresses them.
//
// Detached work is not a notice that something is happening elsewhere. A
// detached agent is a live, growing, recursively spawning conversation; a
// workflow is a live table of journal rows; a backgrounded shell command is a
// live byte spool. Each is modeled as what it is.
//
// IT IS A MESSAGE, AND ONLY A MESSAGE. Detached work has identity, provenance,
// containment, a timestamp and content that accumulates — which is the
// definition of a Message and not of a second, parallel kind of thing. So it
// carries NO id of its own (the containing Message's uuid IS its id) and NO
// parent pointer of its own (feed.proto's MessageLineage is the one containment
// relation). What lives here is the PAYLOAD: what kind of work it is, what it
// is called, whether it is still running, and what it has produced.
//
// AN UPDATE IS NOT A MESSAGE. Work that accumulates output produces bytes and
// emissions, not new feed rows: a shell writing a megabyte would otherwise mint
// thousands of top-level-eligible messages and a page of ten would count them.
// An update is therefore an UPDATE TO AN EXISTING MESSAGE, addressed by that
// message's id — it has no id of its own, no lineage, and never becomes a
// Message. See DetachedWorkUpdate, and DetachedWorkDelta in feed.proto for the
// two-armed channel that carries whole messages and updates side by side.
//
// The daemon owns the whole apparatus: it classifies a detaching tool call,
// mints the message the work is carried by, reads and folds the work's output,
// and pushes typed updates addressed to that message's id. Frontends render
// what arrives; they parse no transcripts, poll no files and derive no
// identities.
